00:45.280 --> 00:48.080
This is an intermediate Python course.

00:48.080 --> 00:52.160
You might also see it referred to as day two throughout the course website.

00:52.160 --> 00:55.280
During this course, we're going to start getting more comfortable

00:55.280 --> 00:58.720
with some of the more advanced aspects of Python,

00:58.720 --> 01:02.880
like classes, list-comprehensions, and the standard library.

01:03.600 --> 01:07.920
At the end of the day, you're going to know enough to build a small interactive

01:07.920 --> 01:10.480
website using the Flask web framework.

01:11.760 --> 01:14.960
Know that to fully participate in today's class,

01:14.960 --> 01:21.360
you must have VS Code set up locally, along with a Python version greater than 3.7.

01:21.920 --> 01:26.560
You must be familiar with all of the concepts from introduction to Python,

01:26.560 --> 01:29.440
which you might see referred to as day one.

01:31.280 --> 01:34.640
Clicking through to the intro to Python section.

01:35.200 --> 01:38.160
You should be familiar with Python conventions, like Pepe,

01:39.280 --> 01:42.800
basic data types, and variables, numbers, strings,

01:43.440 --> 01:47.200
the common errors, and common mistakes you might make as a Python developer.

01:48.080 --> 01:52.400
Functions defining them arguments, function scope.

01:53.440 --> 01:59.840
The more advanced types of Python, the list, the tuple, the set, and the dictionary,

01:59.840 --> 02:07.360
you must be familiar with mutability, and know that a tuple is a immutable data structure,

02:07.360 --> 02:10.080
while list set and dictionary is mutable.

02:11.040 --> 02:18.240
You should know about Boolean logic, the truthiness of a value in Python,

02:18.240 --> 02:25.120
as well as how to make comparisons, such as greater than less than, and end or not,

02:25.120 --> 02:30.480
those Boolean operators. You should know how to loop along with control statements.

02:30.480 --> 02:39.120
You should know the if statement, else four loops, wall loops, and break,

02:39.200 --> 02:44.080
continue, and return. Lastly, you should know how to work with Python programs,

02:44.640 --> 02:50.560
how to run them, what the main method is, exceptions, and tracebacks, and how to

02:51.520 --> 02:57.600
work with simple libraries. Lastly, you should know a little bit about what an API is,

02:57.600 --> 03:04.720
and how to work with them, requests, and responses, HTTP status codes, and how to use the request

03:04.800 --> 03:12.320
library and Python. If you're not sure if you know these topics, I would suggest that you go ahead

03:12.320 --> 03:18.000
and watch the intro to Python course first. You're going to get a lot more value out of intermediate

03:18.000 --> 03:23.520
Python if you have a solid understanding of these foundational concepts. If you're choosing

03:23.520 --> 03:30.160
to join us for intermediate Python without doing the intro Python course first, go ahead and scroll

03:30.240 --> 03:36.000
up to the top of the course website, click on the course introduction, and then go to the

03:36.000 --> 03:41.520
requirements settings. You'll have to go through all of these steps to be able to follow along

03:41.520 --> 03:46.480
with the exercises in intermediate Python.

